# Greaseweazle disk definition for the Thomson TO8 3.5" 640 KB media, as the
# builder's fd640 storage writes it :
#   80 cylinders, 2 sides, 16 sectors of 256 bytes, MFM 250 kbit/s, 300 rpm,
#   physical interleave 7 (the DOS format numbering, storage.xml hardskip="7").
#
# Why not the stock thomson.2s320 : Thomson treats each side as its own drive
# and formats BOTH sides with head number 0 in the sector ID field (toje /
# teo-wd : MFM_HEAD_NUMBER = 0), and the THMFC1 checks that byte against the
# head bit the monitor programs. The stock definition writes H=1 on side 1,
# so a real TO8 finds no sector there — "I/O Error" at boot, the loader's tail
# living on side 1 of track 0 (07/09/2026, two disks written and verified on
# a PC drive, both refused by the TO8).
#
#   gw write --diskdefs thomson-to8.diskdefs --format thomson.to8 to8.fd
#   gw read  --diskdefs thomson-to8.diskdefs --format thomson.to8 relu.fd
#
# The .fd image is side 0 whole then side 1 whole, which Greaseweazle's FD
# reader already expects. An HD diskette in a PC drive needs its HD hole
# taped over, or the DD write does not take.
disk thomson.to8
    cyls = 80
    heads = 2
    tracks * ibm.mfm
        secs = 16
        bps = 256
        rate = 250
        interleave = 7
        h = 0
    rpm = 300
    end
end
